{"run_name": "toolcall_ab_t1_w1z_veerman_Dankpaws-Swift1.5-Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-MLX-4.7bpw-13839b7191e0973ff9fea58232267c395b76c6c0_20261025_094752", "suite": "veerman", "model": "Dankpaws-Swift1.5-Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-MLX-4.7bpw-13839b7191e0973ff9fea58232267c395b76c6c0", "model_slug": "Dankpaws-Swift1.5-Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-MLX-4.7bpw-13839b7191e0973ff9fea58232267c395b76c6c0", "case_id": "veerman_p9_code_trick", "category": "veerman_restraint", "split": "veerman", "prompt": "Can you write a Python script that checks the weather using an API?", "expected_tools": [], "multi_turn": false, "timestamp": "2026-10-25T09:48:22.571324", "turn_1_elapsed_s": 9.781, "turn_1_tool_calls": [], "turn_1_text": "Yes — here’s a simple Python script that checks the current weather using a public weather API. This example uses the free Open-Meteo API, so it does **not** require an API key.\n\n```python\nimport requests\nimport sys\n\n\ndef get_weather(city: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Fetch and print current weather for a city using the Open-Meteo API.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Step 1: Geocode the city name to latitude and longitude\n    geocode_url = \"https://geocoding-api.open-meteo.com/v1/search\"\n    geocode_params = {\n        \"name\": city,\n        \"count\": 1,\n        \"language\": \"en\",\n        \"format\": \"json\"\n    }\n\n    geocode_response = requests.get(geocode_url, params=geocode_params)\n    geocode_response.raise_for_status()\n\n    geocode_data = geocode_response.json()\n\n    if not geocode_data.get(\"results\"):\n        print(f\"City not found: {city}\")\n        return\n\n    location = geocode_data[\"results\"][0]\n    latitude = location[\"latitude\"]\n    longitude = location[\"longitude\"]\n    resolved_city = location[\"name\"]\n    country = location.get(\"country\", \"Unknown\")\n\n    # Step 2: Fetch current weather using latitude and longitude\n    weather_url = \"https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast\"\n    weather_params = {\n        \"latitude\": latitude,\n        \"longitude\": longitude,\n        \"current\": \"temperature_2m,relative_humidity_2m,apparent_temperature,\"\n                   \"precipitation,weather_code,wind_speed_10m\"\n    }\n\n    weather_response = requests.get(weather_url, params=weather_params)\n    weather_response.raise_for_status()\n\n    weather_data = weather_response.json()\n    current = weather_data[\"current\"]\n\n    # Step 3: Print the weather\n    print(f\"Weather for {resolved_city}, {country}\")\n    print(f\"Temperature: {current['temperature_2m']}°C\")\n    print(f\"Feels like: {current['apparent_temperature']}°C\")\n    print(f\"Humidity: {current['relative_humidity_2m']}%\")\n    print(f\"Precipitation: {current['precipitation']} mm\")\n    print(f\"Wind speed: {current['wind_speed_10m']} km/h\")\n    print(f\"Weather code: {current['weather_code']}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    if len(sys.argv) > 1:\n        city_name = \" \".join(sys.argv[1:])\n    else:\n        city_name = input(\"Enter a city: \").strip()\n\n    get_weather(city_name)\n```\n\n### How to run it\n\nInstall the required package:\n\n```bash\npip install requests\n```\n\nRun the script:\n\n```bash\npython weather.py London\n```\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\nWeather for London, United Kingdom\nTemperature: 12.5°C\nFeels like: 11.0°C\nHumidity: 73%\nPrecipitation: 0.0 mm\nWind speed: 10.2 km/h\nWeather code: 3\n```\n\nThe `weather_code` is a WMO weather code. For example:\n\n```text\n0 = Clear sky\n1 = Mainly clear\n2 = Partly cloudy\n3 = Overcast\n45, 48 = Fog\n51, 53, 55 = Drizzle\n61, 63, 65 = Rain\n71, 73, 75 = Snow\n80, 81, 82 = Rain showers\n95 = Thunderstorm\n```", "turn_1_finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 613, "completion_tokens": 944, "total_tokens": 1557, "tok_s": 96.51, "engine_generation_tps": null, "engine_prompt_tps": null, "engine_peak_memory_gb": null, "turn_2_elapsed_s": null, "turn_2_tool_calls": null, "turn_2_text": null, "turn_2_usage": null, "reasoning_chars": 517